    "body": "By Charlotte Goldstone\n23 November 2026\nShipping faces additional regulatory burdens, higher freight costs, and prolonged uncertainty if governments fail to agree meaningful economic measures this month, warned maritime emissions specialists.\nAhead of the International Maritime Organization (IMO) Marine Environment Protection Committee meeting (MEPC 85), in London from 30 November to 4 December, OceanScore MD Albrecht Grell warned that negotiations focusing on technical measures could undermine investment in cleaner fuels and vessels.\n“If only technical measures can be agreed, more measuring, tracking, and reporting work will be forced upon shipping without any real progress towards net-zero shipping,” he told The Loadstar .\n“My concern is that the discussion increasingly centres around technical measures. Standalone, these are not helpful – think of CII,” he added, referring to the IMO’s Carbon Intensity Indicator.\nMr Grell argued that economic measures were essential to stimulating investment in alternative-fuel production, fleet renewal, and fuel-ready vessels. He pointed to the impact of the EU Emissions Trading System (ETS) and FuelEU Maritime, which he estimated affected only around 30% of the global fleet.\n“A meaningful IMO Net-Zero Framework (NZF) could help to close that gap,” he said.\nHowever, failure to agree economic measures could delay an effective IMO NFZ for a prolonged period, potentially encouraging more countries to introduce their own emissions regulations, he added.\nHis comments follow a joint appeal from nine shipping associations, including BIMCO, the International Chamber of Shipping (ICS) and World Shipping Council (WSC), urging governments to establish a global greenhouse gas framework providing investment certainty and preventing overlapping regional requirements.\nFriederike Hesse, co-founder and MD of maritime emissions management specialist zero44, told The Loadstar that although IMO member states had become “more constructive”, a final agreement was more likely in 2027 than at December’s meeting.\nShe highlighted that governments had previously converged on several principles, including prioritising emissions reductions, allowing ships to trade compliance surpluses and incentivising zero-emission fuels. However, disagreements remained over the ambition and duration of emissions targets and how revenues would be used.\nMs Hesse warned that continued uncertainty would carry direct commercial consequences, adding: “Uncertainty itself is a cost. Companies price-in risk premiums, put their eggs in multiple baskets instead of focusing investments, add contractual buffers, and spend more on legal and administrative work – and that ends up in freight rates.”\nShe also warned that delaying emissions reductions would require steeper cuts later, potentially leaving fuel production and fleet renewal struggling to keep pace.\n“Postponing cuts doesn’t make them disappear,” she urged, warning that the resulting transition could become more expensive and risk disruption to global trade.\nFor shipowners and fuel suppliers making investments over 20 to 30 years, regulatory uncertainty could discourage fuel offtake agreements and slow alternative-fuel production.\nMr Grell argued that decarbonisation costs would ultimately fall on cargo owners, although operators’ ability to adapt would determine the impact on profitability.\n“Shipping is highly competitive – any cost will eventually be passed on to the cargo owners,” he said. “The profit pool will not change but be redistributed within the industry.”\nBoth experts warned against proliferating regional schemes without effective global regulation. Ms Hesse said this could concentrate alternative-fuel infrastructure around regulated markets, rather than global trade routes, while exposing operators to overlapping compliance obligations.\nMr Grell agreed: “Nobody benefits from overlapping reporting regimes, admin layers and compliance efforts.”\nAccording to Ms Hesse, regional measures such as the EU ETS should complement an eventual IMO framework, potentially recognising payments already made under global rules.\n“Whether regional schemes disappear entirely matters less than making sure no tonne of CO₂ is charged twice,” she said.",
